Where Experts Disagree
1. Weekly Publishing vs. Research-Intensive Publishing
Expert A: Jason Lemkin
Jason Lemkin recommends publishing high-quality content consistently. His approach prioritises maintaining visibility and creating regular opportunities to educate prospects and customers.
Sources
Jason Lemkin. 10 Things That Always Work in Marketing. SaaStr. 2024.
https://www.saastr.com/10-things-that-always-work-in-marketing-with-saastr-founder-and-ceo-jason-lemkin-part-1-pod-652-video/
Jason Lemkin. Scaling from $1M to $10M: An AMA. SaaStr. 2023.
https://www.saastr.com/scaling-from-1-to-10m-an-ama-with-saastr-ceo-and-founder-jason-lemkin-pod-573/
Expert B: Mario Gabriele
Mario Gabriele publishes fewer, highly researched pieces. His model prioritises originality, depth, and authority over frequent output.
Source
Mario Gabriele. No Rivals: The Prophet — Founders Fund, Part I. The Generalist. 12 June 2025.
https://www.generalist.com/p/founders-fund-1
Which Side I Take and Why
I support Mario Gabriele’s approach for newsletters aimed at intermediate and expert audiences.
These readers are more likely to value original research and defensible analysis than frequent commentary. However, the model requires significant time and expertise. Smaller teams may need to balance Mario’s depth with Jason’s consistency.
2. Storytelling vs. Structured Clarity
Expert A: Packy McCormick
Packy McCormick uses narrative, personality, and strong editorial perspectives to make complex business and technology topics engaging.
Sources
Packy McCormick. Story Time: The Progressive Decentralization of Narrative. Not Boring. 16 August 2021.
https://www.notboring.co/p/story-time
Packy McCormick. Tell Good Stories. Not Boring. 14 February 2023.
https://www.notboring.co/p/tell-good-stories
Expert B: Emily Kramer
Based on recurring patterns in Emily Kramer’s work, I infer that she teaches marketing through structured frameworks, systems, and practical implementation guides.
This is an interpretation of her publishing approach, not an explicit rejection of storytelling.
Sources
Emily Kramer. MKT1 Guide to Annual Marketing Planning. MKT1 Newsletter. 23 September 2024.
https://newsletter.mkt1.co/p/annual-marketing-planning
Emily Kramer. MKT1 Buildathon: How to Use Our MCP in Claude. MKT1 Newsletter. Publication date not listed.
https://newsletter.mkt1.co/p/buildathon-mkt1-mcp-in-claude
Which Side I Take and Why
I support Packy McCormick’s storytelling approach for thought leadership, category creation, and emerging technologies.
Stories can make unfamiliar ideas easier to understand and remember. However, I would combine storytelling with Emily Kramer’s structured frameworks so readers leave with practical takeaways.
3. Broad Distribution vs. Commercial Relevance
Expert A: Kyle Poyar
Kyle Poyar recommends using LinkedIn, original research, community engagement, and zero-click content to build organic reach and attract newsletter subscribers.
Source
Kyle Poyar. LinkedIn Isn’t Over. You’re Just Bad at It. Growth Unhinged. 25 June 2025.
https://www.growthunhinged.com/p/guide-to-mastering-linkedin
Expert B: Jason Lemkin
Jason Lemkin prioritises creating valuable content for relevant prospects and customers rather than maximising audience size without commercial relevance.
Source
Jason Lemkin. An Underused Strategy: Truly High Value Drip Marketing to Prospects. SaaStr. 10 June 2019.
https://www.saastr.com/an-underused-strategy-truly-high-value-drip-marketing-to-prospects/
Which Side I Take and Why
I support Kyle Poyar’s approach during the early stage.
Broad organic distribution can help an emerging company build category awareness, founder credibility, and a larger pool of potential customers. As the company matures, I would shift towards Jason Lemkin’s emphasis on commercially relevant readers and conversion.
Decision Context
For an early-stage B2B SaaS company, my priorities are:
Build authority through original insights.
Build a recognisable voice through storytelling.
Build awareness through organic distribution.
These priorities should evolve as the newsletter and company mature.
What I Rejected and Why
1. High-Volume Publishing as the Default
Idea Rejected
Publishing across many channels at a high frequency to maximise visibility.
Why I Rejected It
Small teams may struggle to maintain quality and originality at a high cadence. I recommend beginning with weekly or fortnightly publishing and increasing frequency only when the process is sustainable.
I am rejecting high-volume publishing as the default, not consistency itself.
Sources
Jason Lemkin. 10 Things That Always Work in Marketing. SaaStr. 2024.
https://www.saastr.com/10-things-that-always-work-in-marketing-with-saastr-founder-and-ceo-jason-lemkin-part-1-pod-652-video/
Jason Lemkin. Scaling from $1M to $10M: An AMA. SaaStr. 2023.
https://www.saastr.com/scaling-from-1-to-10m-an-ama-with-saastr-ceo-and-founder-jason-lemkin-pod-573/
2. Extremely Long Research Reports as the Default
Idea Rejected
Using highly detailed, research-intensive reports as the standard newsletter format.
Why I Rejected It
This format can build authority but requires extensive time, research, and subject-matter expertise. Most B2B SaaS companies also need consistent communication with prospects and customers.
I would reserve this format for major strategic topics or flagship reports.
Source
Mario Gabriele. No Rivals: The Prophet — Founders Fund, Part I. The Generalist. 12 June 2025.
https://www.generalist.com/p/founders-fund-1
My Original Ideas
1. Gamifying the Newsletter Experience
Idea
Design the newsletter as an interactive learning journey rather than a one-way communication channel.
Each edition could include:
One practical mission
Progress tracking
Seasonal learning paths
Completion milestones
Downloadable resources
Optional community challenges
Why It Could Work
The approach could encourage readers to apply what they learn rather than consume information passively. Visible progress and practical missions may also strengthen learning habits and long-term engagement.
Note: This is a hypothesis that would need to be tested through reader behaviour and feedback.
2. Applying Loss Aversion to Newsletter Retention
Idea
Use progress reminders to encourage readers to complete a learning series.
Examples:
“You have completed four of six lessons.”
“You are one lesson away from completing the Positioning Season.”
“The next issue includes the final implementation framework.”
Why It Could Work
Readers who have already invested time in a structured series may be more motivated to complete it. Progress reminders could strengthen return behaviour and series completion.
This idea is an untested application of behavioural design to B2B newsletters.
Note: Both ideas require experimentation before they can be treated as standard practice.
Weaknesses of This Playbook
The Complete System Has Not Been Tested
The individual recommendations are informed by expert guidance, but this exact combination has not been tested as one integrated system.
Success Depends on Execution
The playbook cannot compensate for weak writing, poor audience understanding, inconsistent execution, or low-value content.
Different Business Models
The experts operate across premium media, SaaS, creator businesses, startup marketing, and technical publishing. Their recommendations may not transfer equally across every context.
Some Conclusions Are Inferences
Not every source provides direct newsletter instructions. Some conclusions are based on patterns in how the experts create, organise, and distribute their own content.
Sources Reviewed but Not Prioritised
One expert from the broader research was reviewed but excluded from the primary analysis above.
Gergely Orosz
Gergely Orosz produces high-quality work on software engineering, engineering leadership, and the technology industry.
However, I would not prioritise him as a primary source for someone learning B2B SaaS newsletter and email marketing because the material reviewed contained limited direct guidance on:
Newsletter growth
Subscriber acquisition
Email marketing
Content distribution
Newsletter monetisation
His work remains highly valuable for engineering leadership, technical communication, and publishing for expert technical audiences.
